<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Regensburg</h1><p class="st-meta">1250 CE – 1803 CE (approx.) · capital Regensburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small Danube church state of the bishops of Regensburg, who governed a great Bavarian diocese from a Gothic cathedral but ruled only a little land.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-boniface">Saint Boniface</a> organised the diocese of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-regensburg">Regensburg</a> in 739, building on an older <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s seat in the Roman city. Its bishops were overshadowed in the city by the Bavarian dukes and the abbey of St. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-emmeram">Emmeram</a>, but gathered scattered lordships such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donaustauf">Donaustauf</a>, Wörth and Hohenburg, which they ruled as imperial princes from the 13th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s secular territory was small and fragmented, while his diocese covered much of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. The scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albertus-magnus">Albertus Magnus</a> served briefly as bishop in 1260 to 1262. The Gothic cathedral of St. Peter was built from 1273 over more than two centuries. The see remained Catholic, while the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-regensburg">Free Imperial City of Regensburg</a> around it turned Lutheran, and many bishops came from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">Wittelsbach</a> house.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1803 the prince-bishopric was merged into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/principality-of-regensburg">Principality of Regensburg</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-theodor-von-dalberg">Karl Theodor von Dalberg</a>, which passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in 1810. The diocese continues, and its cathedral choir is world famous.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-bishopric-of-regensburg.jpg" alt="Prince-Bishopric of Regensburg" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: MostEpic · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banner_of_the_Prince-Bishopric_of_Regensburg_(1%5E1).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1250 CE – 1803 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Regensburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Albertus Magnus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bavarian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hochstift Regensburg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1526&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-regensburg" aria-label="Prince-Bishopric of Regensburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4959 467 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Prince-Bishopric of Regensburg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5279 723L5229 694M5279 723L5279 723"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5279 723h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5279 723h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5279 723h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Regensburg. <a href="/atlas?year=1526&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-regensburg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
